    <GaugingStation location="Boat o Brig" name="Spey" number="8006">
        <Comments>
            <Comment type="Station Summary">Velocity area station, the most downstream station on the Spey.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Hydrometric">Natural control. Floods above 2.5m bypass station on lb through arches of railway bridge, but this only represents a small percentage of the overall flow. Peak flow ratings derived from current meter gaugings, simple extrapolation beyond. Rating has been derived using only post-2009 gaugings and was revised in 2016.</Comment>
            <Comment type="General">Velocity area station 65m wide. Cableway rated. Lowest station currently operating on the Spey.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow record">Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in April 2017 (WINFAP Files v5). Chart data between 1953 &amp; 1986 was digitised in 2016.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow regime">Storage at Loch Insh and Insh marshes - at high flows floodplain becomes part of loch. 380 sq km developed for hydro-power with diversions and storage; limited net impact on annual runoff (small loss). Reservoirs used for hydropower: Spey Dam (1944), Loch an T-Seilich (1941) and Cuaich (1961). Water use also for aluminium industry.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Catchment">Mountainous catchment (includes all north slopes of Cairngorms), often snowy in winter. Bedrock is predominantly Moinian metamorphics with granite intrusions. Dalradian with a little Old Red Sandstone. Landuse is moorland, hill grazing, forest cover, with limited arable land downstream. Cuaich Dam impounded during period of record (1961).</Comment>
            <Comment type="Factors affecting runoff">H</Comment>
        </Comments>
